WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.766
A printer can follow a perfect toolpath while the

00:00:03.766 --> 00:00:07.244
deposited material collapses. The rheology of the ink

00:00:07.244 --> 00:00:08.228
sets the rules.

00:00:12.217 --> 00:00:15.855
The printing material must move through a nozzle, then

00:00:15.855 --> 00:00:19.354
retain enough structure after deposition to realise

00:00:19.354 --> 00:00:19.560
the

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.503
intended form.

00:00:24.717 --> 00:00:28.273
The forthcoming chapter considers three-, four-, five-

00:00:28.273 --> 00:00:31.764
and six-dimensional food-printing concepts, including

00:00:31.764 --> 00:00:34.859
responsive structures and programmed behaviour.

00:00:39.758 --> 00:00:43.612
Composition, texture and printing conditions interact.

00:00:43.612 --> 00:00:43.683
A

00:00:43.683 --> 00:00:47.257
promising design requires material-specific testing

00:00:47.257 --> 00:00:49.710
before its properties are asserted.

00:00:54.508 --> 00:00:58.115
Time-responsive and higher-dimensional labels describe

00:00:58.115 --> 00:01:01.448
proposed capabilities; they do not establish that

00:01:01.448 --> 00:01:01.788
every

00:01:01.788 --> 00:01:04.726
structure has been practically demonstrated.

00:01:09.675 --> 00:01:13.409
This accepted chapter explores the design constraints

00:01:13.409 --> 00:01:13.620
for

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:17.358
programmable edible materials. It remains forthcoming,

00:01:17.358 --> 00:01:19.711
not a completed laboratory result.
